const body = `<h1>Design From a Spec</h1>

<p>
    Free-response Question 2 hands you a scenario, a specification table, and
    says: implement. No starter code beyond maybe a shell. The spec is the
    documentation, and you are the implementer. This page is the professional
    loop for that moment, scaled to classroom size.
</p>

<h2>The Loop</h2>

<ol>
    <li><strong>Read the spec twice.</strong> Once for the story, once for the
    contract. Underline every noun (candidate fields) and every verb (candidate
    methods).</li>
    <li><strong>Plan on paper.</strong> Fields with types, constructor, method
    list with signatures. Do not open the editor yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Implement bottom-up or top-down, your call.</strong> Bottom-up:
    fields, constructor, accessors, then the brainy methods. Top-down: stubs
    first, then fill.</li>
    <li><strong>Build the acceptance table.</strong> Every spec row becomes a
    test line with a predictable output.</li>
    <li><strong>Run, compare, fix.</strong> One row at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Review against the spec.</strong> Types, edges, formats,
    privacy. Another pair of eyes if you can get them.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Reading a Spec Table Like the Exam Writes Them</h2>

<pre><code>A BankAccount has an owner, a balance, and an interest rate.
Constructor: BankAccount(String owner, double balance, double rate)

deposit(double amount)      adds amount to balance
withdraw(double amount)     removes amount if funds suffice; returns
                            true on success, false otherwise
getBalance()                returns the current balance
monthlyInterest()           adds balance * rate / 12 to balance
toString()                  "Aram: $142.50"</code></pre>

<p>Nouns: owner, balance, rate (three fields, types String, double, double).
Verbs: deposit, withdraw (boolean!), monthlyInterest (void, it mutates),
getBalance (double), toString (String). The return type column is where specs
hide their tests: "returns true on success, false otherwise" is a boolean
method, and the exam will absolutely test the boundary case, withdrawing
exactly the balance.</p>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Spec words that carry hidden tests:</strong>
    "at most" (means &lt;=, test the equal case), "if funds suffice" (test
    exactly-equal funds), "otherwise" (there is an else branch, test it),
    "returns true/false" (boolean, test both), "in order" (the sequence
    matters), "first occurrence" (index 0 exists and duplicates might not
    count). When you read these words, write the edge test before the code.
</div>

<h2>Worked Example: The Spell Contract</h2>

<p>The 5.6a lesson builds Spell from a table. Here is the harder variant that
the lesson saves for the challenge, worked in full. Spec:</p>

<pre><code>A Potion has a name, a price in gold, and a potency (1-5).
Constructor: Potion(String name, int price, int potency)

getName()                   the name
getPrice()                  the price
getPotency()                the potency
isLuxury()                  true when price is over 100 gold
discountedPrice(int pct)    returns price minus pct percent, as a double,
                            truncated to whole gold
toString()                  "Elixir of Might (120g, potency 3)"</code></pre>

<p>Planning, on paper, before code:</p>

<pre><code>fields: private String name; private int price; private int potency;
constructor: assign all three
isLuxury: return price &gt; 100;                      // "over" = strict &gt;
discountedPrice: return (int)(price * (100 - pct) / 100.0);   // watch the cast
toString: name + " (" + price + "g, potency " + potency + ")";</code></pre>

<p>The two traps in the plan: "over 100" is <code>&gt;</code> not
<code>&gt;=</code>, and discountedPrice is where integer division and casting
(1.8a) come back. <code>price * (100 - pct) / 100.0</code> with the 100.0
double keeps the math honest; the outer (int) truncates as specified.</p>

<h2>Writing Specs (the other direction)</h2>

<p>Design is writing the table. Test your table by handing it to someone. If
they build something different from what you imagined, your spec was ambiguous,
not their fault. Common ambiguities to hunt in your own specs:</p>

<ul>
    <li>Unstated types ("price": int or double?)</li>
    <li>Unstated boundaries ("big": how big exactly?)</li>
    <li>Unstated returns (does withdraw report failure, or just do nothing?)</li>
    <li>Unstated format (toString exact punctuation)</li>
    <li>Unstated mutation (does monthlyInterest change balance or just report?)</li>
</ul>

<h2>Tinker Challenge: Spec Tennis</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Trade Tables With a Partner</h3>
    <ol>
        <li>Both write a spec table for an RPG class with at least 4 methods
        (your choice: Trap, Mount, Quest, Scroll, Enchantment).</li>
        <li>Swap. Build each other's class WITHOUT asking questions. If the spec
        is ambiguous, write your assumption as a comment and keep going.</li>
        <li>Swap back. Review what they built. Every place their assumption
        differed from your intent is a bug IN YOUR SPEC.</li>
        <li>Revise your spec table and archive the diff. The revision is the
        lesson.</li>
    </ol>
</div>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why does the exam test spec-reading instead of open design?</h3>
    <p>Because grading needs a right answer, and because reading a contract
    faithfully is the larger half of professional programming. Most code is
    written to someone else's spec. The creative version, spec-writing, is why
    5.6a makes you do both directions.</p>
    <h3>Why plan on paper first?</h3>
    <p>Because the editor rewards typing and punishes thinking. Paper has no
    autocomplete, no red squiggles, no half-working code luring you forward.
    90 seconds of planning routinely saves 20 minutes of fixing. Every working
    programmer has a story about the time they skipped the plan.</p>
</div>

